{
	"_comment": "Čeština. Short passive participle register — exactly how Czech software phrases outcomes: 'Soubor smazán', 'Větev vytvořena'. Agreement is add-only ({f: add a, n: add o}) rather than strip-and-add: Czech short participles already end on the bare consonant (smazán, vytvořen, opraven), so the feminine/neuter just weld a vowel on; this covers all 27 verbs here with no per-verb past_f overrides needed. Gerund slot is the imperfective verbal noun in -ání/-ení/-ování (Mazání..., Spouštění..., Instalování...) — the -ovat → -ování derivation is fully productive so it is applied uniformly, even in a couple of spots (instalování, aktualizování, kontrolování) where a shorter deverbal noun (instalace, aktualizace, kontrola) is at least as common in real UI chrome; both are correct, the gerund form was preferred here for pattern consistency with the rest of the table. One deliberately flagged register choice: push → pushnout/pushnut/pushování is a developer-slang anglicism (Czech devs say 'pushnul jsem to'), not formal written Czech — chosen on the same precedent as this engine's own de.json ('pushen') and nl.json ('gepusht') entries for the identical git concept, where no native verb has taken over the slot. No articles (article.none). Nominative-only v1, accepted deliberately: real Czech wants the accusative object after a transitive verb ('pushnout' + větev wants accusative, which happens to equal the nominative for this feminine i-stem noun) — no case declension is attempted anywhere in this file. Plural resolution is binary (one/other) per the engine's Pluralize(); Czech's own CLDR few/many split (2-4 vs 5+) is not modelled here — 'other' covers every count above 1, same simplification as the case caveat.",
